[THOUGHT] Alcohol abstinence, no smoking, and no recent international travel significantly reduce likelihood of alcoholic cirrhosis, smoking-related malignancy (e.g., lung, gastric), or travel-associated infections (e.g., schistosomiasis, TB). Next, domain #7: targeted physical examination. Given prominent abdominal distension and exertional dyspnea, key exam elements include:  
- Vital signs (especially BP, HR, RR, O₂ saturation, JVP) — to assess for volume overload, hypoxia, or elevated central venous pressure;  
- Cardiac exam (S3, murmurs, distant heart sounds) — for heart failure or pericardial disease;  
- Pulmonary exam (crackles, wheezes, decreased breath sounds) — for pulmonary edema or effusion;  
- Abdominal exam (shifting dullness, fluid wave, hepatosplenomegaly, tenderness, masses) — to confirm ascites and screen for underlying liver or intra-abdominal pathology;  
- Extremities (edema, clubbing, cyanosis) — though patient denied leg swelling, objective assessment is needed.  
Jugular venous pressure (JVP) is especially critical: elevated JVP supports right heart failure, constrictive pericarditis, or tricuspid regurgitation — all of which can cause ascites + dyspnea without peripheral edema. So I’ll request JVP assessment first as a high-yield, non-invasive vital sign–adjacent exam.

[TEST] Jugular venous pressure (JVP) measurement

